# ---------------------------------------------------------------- build and run: English bullets
D.append("- **Dependencies.**", " A workspace package created after the last sync (a new `package.json`) cannot be resolved until `dependencies lock --all` and `dependencies sync --all` ran; every route that imports it fails with `Cannot find module` until then (seen on 2026-10-20 with a new framework package, which stopped the `verify` routes and the documentation-law tests). Sync cost: the verification passes were quadratic in the number of bindings. The installed-paths scan now compares a directory with itself when it closes instead of re-reading every ancestor of every entry (progress frames every `scanProgressWorkUnits` = 4,096 work units), the publisher re-checks only the strict ancestors of an endpoint's parent (`javascriptDependencyStrictAncestorsV1`), each published binding gets a 12-`lstat` endpoint fence (`requireJavascriptDependencyEndpointFenceV1`), and the complete fence runs every `fullFenceInterval` = 32 bindings and after the last one (constants in " + INSTALL + "). Measured on the primary host at load 31 to 52: the scan of one 84,345-entry generation fell from 168 to 302 s to 48 to 64 s, and the last green `dependencies sync --all` (2026-10-20 05:43) took about 31 minutes, where an earlier attempt ran 7,000 s without finishing. Leave the tree alone while a sync runs: a peer edit of a provider file during the walk failed one attempt with `Declared execution physical observation changed`. The suites that pin these fences are the targets `repo:test-javascript-installed-paths`, `repo:test-javascript-endpoint-fence` and `repo:test-javascript-provider-walk` (project `repo`, directory `⚡️caching`). Recorded on 2026-10-20 on the live tree under an issued envelope: the endpoint-fence suite passed (5 tests), the provider-walk suite failed its table test on the row `provider-relinked-in-first-replay` (expected refused; not diagnosed, open), and the installed-paths suite could not load because the installed generation predates the package `@semio-tech/framework-filesystem` that it imports.", "en")

D.insert_after("- **Dependencies.**", [
"- **Dependency generation receipt.** Every `bun repo` task or graph call first proves that the installed dependencies still match their sources. That proof used to be a complete walk of the workspace (eight directory-tree passes of about 617,000 operations and four provider walks per command, 36 to 58 s per tree pass under load). It is now a sealed receipt per dependency scope, checked cheapest tier first (`Protocol.tiers` in `📚️library/⚡️caching/🚀️bootstrap/📦️dependencies/🧾️generation/🧬️schema/🔣️.json`): `implementation` (content digest of every file in the runtime import closure of the walk and fence entries, read with `captureBuildSourceClosureV1`; 175 files, 2.1 MB on the measured tree), `runtime` (Bun and Node versions, Bun revision, platform, installer recipe), `producer` (the `.npmrc` and `bunfig.toml` files an installer level could read, and the `BUN_*`, `NPM_CONFIG_*`, proxy and certificate variables, by name and value digest only), `bindings`, `inputs` (manifests, lock, bunfig, patches), `membership` (every walked directory by stamp; a directory younger than 5 s is listed again and compared by projection digest) and `execution` (every physical observation the provider walk made, replayed). Any difference is a typed mismatch (`Protocol.mismatchClasses`) and the command falls back to the complete walk, which then passes or throws exactly as before. Receipts and their evidence are content-addressed files in `.cache/b/d/m/generation`; verification runs under the caller's shared store custody and publishes progress `dependencies:generation-verify:<n>`. Only an `inspect` with exclusive custody and the end of a `dependencies sync` publish a receipt (`publishJavascriptDependencyGenerationReceiptsElseV1`; best effort, a failure prints `[DATA]` and never fails the sync), and a capture defers while any closure file is younger than the process start minus 5 s, so a receipt only vouches for code that ran. Consequence: an edit of any file of the closure (126 of the 175 are process modules) costs the next command one complete walk. Measured read-only on the primary host at load 56 to 65: a complete receipt verifies in 3.6 to 12 s against 36 to 58 s for one tree pass; the closure and producer tiers add about 10 ms. Not yet verified: on 2026-10-20 the generation directory held three zero-byte `*.frontier.ndjson` files and no sealed receipt, so no live run has published and then verified one; Linux, Windows and the devcontainer were not run.",
"- **Waiting for a store.** A cache image and other shared resources are claimed through a queue (`acquireQueuedResourceLease`, resource `native-fixed-image:<partition path>`): tickets are served in arrival order, a shared ticket joins a cohort only when every earlier ticket is a shared cohort ticket, so a writer is not starved by readers that arrive after it, and a waiter reaps only its immediate predecessor when that process is dead. Waiters poll from 250 ms to at most 1,000 ms (factor 3/2) and sweep at most once per second (`🧰️framework/🔨️modules/🏃️process/🔒️leases/⏳️wait/🧬️schema/📜️policy.json`); with 14 waiters behind one holder for 15 s their CPU time fell from 15.2 s to 2.2 s. A waiter at position 2 or later publishes every 15 s a `lease-queue` progress line, shown in English or German, for example `Waiting for access to native-fixed-image:<path> (shared): position 3 of 15, behind the request of process 12528 (exclusive, queued 8040 s ago)`: the pid names the holder. A line `<command> remains active on dependencies:store-ownership:state:<N> (exclusive, <s> s)` is the generic progress line of a running walk (N counts work), not a wait. `upgradeQueuedResourceLease` turns a held queued shared lease into an exclusive one at its original queue position and consumes the shared hold; asking exclusively for a resource the same process already holds shared still waits for itself until the timeout. The raw `acquireResourceLease` (polling SQLite) is not queued and prefers readers, so mixing it with queued requesters can starve a queued writer; image paths use the queue unless `maximumLeaseDatabaseBytes` is set, and whether the raw primitive should prefer writers is an open owner decision. A single `bun repo run` that holds an image exclusively for hours (a store ownership walk after a dependency generation change) blocks every other command on that image: read the head ticket before restarting a sync. The target `test-resource-lease-fairness` of `@semio-tech/framework-process` runs the fixture-driven suite (queued Bun and Node clients against a queued writer, Python `sqlite3` readers as the independent client, dead predecessors, CPU cost, progress text, upgrade rules). Recorded: a plain `bun test` of the fairness file on the live tree on 2026-10-20 passed 10 of 11 tests; the failing case (`python-readers-queued-writer`, a timing case against Python readers) passed when run alone, so it is load sensitive. Not yet verified through `bun repo run`: the first check after the suite was applied (2026-10-20 05:05) is recorded as failed and was not repeated through the runner.",
], "en")

D.append("- **Cargo.**", " Before Cargo starts, `bun repo cargo` admits the Source of every compilation unit. The store-custody recheck inside that admission is throttled to one per 15 s (`storageRecheckGateV1`; `storageRecheck.intervalMilliseconds` in the admission schema; the first call and the completion call always recheck), and `rustc --print cfg` is computed once per exact argument vector; on a real image at load about 55 an admission unit with 12 reads dropped from 12.3 s to 3.0 s. A compile that finds the Rust image below the compiler charge (4 GiB) frees space through the recovery in `📚️library/⚡️caching/💾️storage/🔒️enforcement/🏗️compiler/📏️capacity/♻️recovery`, which compares the invocation identity of the control (`requireIssuedResourceControlInvocationV1`), not its signal. After a refactor that touches many crates, run small `bun repo cargo` invocations one after the other, never beside a dependency sync or a graph inference (all three take long store leases); the resumable plan that sequenced the 2026-10-20 recompile window (205 required steps over 644 crates, at most 8 `-p` packages each) is a tool of the coordination ticket, not a `bun repo` command. Not yet verified: when this was written that window had only started its first lock-refresh step, so the value-family, proctor and activation-closure compiles are still unproven.", "en")

D.append("- **Zero-touch.**", " The law `bun ./📜️script.ts verify zero-touch` keeps that closure honest (see Build owner registry).", "en")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- manifests: command source closure
D.insert_after("**Inputs.**", [
"",
"**Command source closure.** A `repo:script` target needs no hand-written list of the modules its script imports. The graph inference derives the closure of the command script (`📚️library/🏗️build/🕸️graph/📥️inference/🧭️script-inputs`): relative imports, imports through workspace package specifiers such as `@semio-tech/process/leases` (`workspacePackageModuleV1`; installed registry packages stay covered by their locked external dependency), literal `new URL(\"…\", import.meta.url)` targets that exist (modules are followed, other files are hashed as resources), and literal module inputs a target declares (for example `{projectRoot}/…/x.test.ts`). The closure joins the target as named inputs `commandSources…`; in the hash representation of the graph it is the marker `{\"env\": \"SEMIO_SOURCE_CLOSURE_<sha256 of the manifest>\"}` and the manifest sits in the graph store under `build-input-closures`, which Nx reads when it hashes the task. Cargo lock projections are markers of the same kind (`SEMIO_CARGO_LOCK_<key>`). Bazel expands the markers itself (see Bazel implementation). Deliberately not covered: in-source test imports guarded by `import.meta.vitest`, files read through `import.meta.dir` or joined string paths, and targets that are never cached (continuous, live). Measured on a sample of 49 project scripts the closure grew from a median of 489 to 1,200 files (about 28 percent more CPU for the derivation, offset by an admission memo that cut the old cost by 35 percent). Not yet verified: the assembled inputs in a real graph load (`bun repo show projects` was stopped on 2026-10-20 after 4 hours; the later analysis found it garbage-collection bound under host memory starvation, not quadratic in the closure code: a project costs 0.3 to 0.45 CPU-s, the closure derivation takes about 29 percent of that, and a clean run is estimated at 25 to 40 minutes), an Nx hash of a real task, and a Bazel run with expanded closures against the production entry.",
], "en")

